      "type": "thinking",
      "thinking": "Let me analyze this OIML Certificate of Conformity for water meters (R49 recommendation).\n\nCertificate details:\n- Certificate number: R49-1/2006-NL1-12.01 revision 1\n- Scheme: not explicitly stated as A or B; OIML-Certificates of Conformity for R49 under the \"basic system\"... Actually there's no explicit scheme mentioned. Omit or use null. The schema says scheme (A|B). Since not stated, I'll omit it. Actually, for R49, it's typically scheme A (basic). But rule 8 says don't fabricate. I'll omit.\n- Project number: 12200373\n- Page total: 7\n- Member state: The Netherlands\n- Date issued: 22 October 2012\n- OIML issuer ID: NL1\n\nIssuing authority:\n- Name: NMi Certin B.V.\n- Address: Hugo de Grootplein 1, 3314 EG Dordrecht, The Netherlands\n- Person responsible: C. Oosterman\n- Person title: Head Certification Board\n- Phone: +31 78 63323232\n- Email: certin@nmi.nl\n- Website: www.nmi.nl\n- OIML issuer ID: NL1\n\nApplicant:\n- KROHNE Altometer, Kerkeplaat 12, 3313 LC Dordrecht, The Netherlands\n\nManufacturer:\n- KROHNE Altometer, Kerkeplaat 12, 3313 LC Dordrecht, The Netherlands\n\nCertified type:\n- Category: Water meter (the category examples include \"Gas meter\"; water meter fits). Actually the R49 is \"Water meters\". Category: \"Water meter\"\n- Type designations: [\"WATERFLUX 3070\"]\n- Module designation: not applicable — omit or \"Not applicable\"? Not mentioned. Omit.\n- Description: \"Water meter intended for the metering of cold potable water and hot water, model \"WATERFLUX 3070\", class 1 and 2.\" Plus further description from page 3: \"Water meter intended for metering cold potable water and hot water, based on an electromagnetic principle, designed to measure forward and reverse flow, with (minimum) 0 D straight inlet and outlet, with no flow conditioner and equipped with an electronic calculating/indicating device.\"\n\nModel family: This is a big matrix. The tables span pages 3-7, covering class 1 (page 3: DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125; page 4: DN150, DN200, DN250, DN300) and class 2 (page 5: DN25, DN40, DN50, DN65; page 6: DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150; page 7: DN200, DN250, DN300).\n\nLet me parse carefully.\n\nPage 3 table (Class 1):\nColumns: Meter size: DN65 (1 col), DN80 (2 cols), DN100 (2 cols), DN125 (2 cols) → total 7 value columns.\n\nRows:\n- Q1 (m³/h): 0.40 | 0.625, 0.64 | 1.0, 1.0 | 1.56, 1.60\n- Q2 (m³/h): 0.64 | 1.00, 1.02 | 1.6, 1.6 | 2.50, 2.56\n- Q3 (m³/h): 100 | 100, 160 | 160, 250 | 250, 400\n- Q4 (m³/h): 125 | 125, 200 | 200, 312.5 | 312.5, 500\n- Ratio Q3/Q1: 250 | 160, 250 | 160, 250 | 160, 250\n- Nominal diameter (mm): 65 | 80 | 100 | 125 (DN80 spans 2 cols, etc.)\n- Accuracy Class: 1 (all)\n- Max admissible pressure (bar): 16 (all)\n- Min/max admissible temp (°C): 0.1/50 (all)\n- Indicating range (m³): 99.999.999 (all)\n- Verification scale interval (m³): 0.0001 (3 cols: DN65 + DN80's 2), 0.001 (4 cols: DN100's 2 + DN125's 2)\n- Orientation: All positions\n- Environmental class: C\n- Power supply: Battery 3.6 V\n- Software versions: 4.0.4_; 4.0.10_; 4.0.11_; 4.0.12_; 4.2.2_; 4.2.4_\n\nSo the variants within DN80: two sub-variants (Q3=100 and Q3=160). The two columns under DN80 represent two variants of DN80: one with Q3=100 m³/h and one with Q3=160 m³/h. Each combination of Q3 and ratio Q3/Q1 forms a variant.\n\nSo the distinct model variants (Class 1):\n1. DN65, Q1=0.40, Q2=0.64, Q3=100, Q4=125, ratio 250\n2. DN80, Q1=0.625, Q2=1.00, Q3=100, Q4=125, ratio 160\n3. DN80, Q1=0.64, Q2=1.02, Q3=160, Q4=200, ratio 250\n4. DN100, Q1=1.0, Q2=1.6, Q3=160, Q4=200, ratio 160\n5. DN100, Q1=1.0, Q2=1.6, Q3=250, Q4=312.5, ratio 250\n6. DN125, Q1=1.56, Q2=2.50, Q3=250, Q4=312.5, ratio 160\n7. DN125, Q1=1.60, Q2=2.56, Q3=400, Q4=500, ratio 250\n\nVerification scale interval: 0.0001 for DN65 and both DN80 variants (first 3 columns); 0.001 for DN100 (both) and DN125 (both) (last 4 columns).\n\nPage 4 (Class 1 continued):\nColumns: DN150 (2 cols), DN200 (1), DN250 (1), DN300 (1) → 5 value columns.\n\nRows:\n- Q1: 2.50, 2.52 | 3.94 | 6.25 | 10\n- Q2: 4.00, 4.03 | 6.30 | 10 | 16\n- Q3: 400 | 630 | 630 | 1000 | 1600\n- Q4: 500 | 787.5 | 787.5 | 1250 | 2000\n- Ratio: 160 | 250 | 160 | 160 | 160\n- Nominal diameter: 150 | 200 | 250 | 300\n- Accuracy class: 1\n- Max pressure: 16 (DN150 both), 10 (DN200, DN250, DN300)\n- Temperature: 0.1/50\n- Indicating range: 99.999.999\n- Verification scale interval: 0.001 (4 cols: DN150 x2, DN200, DN250), 0.01 (DN300)\n- Orientation: All positions\n- Env class: C\n- Power: Battery 3.6 V\n- Software versions: same\n\nClass 1 variants continued:\n8. DN150, Q1=2.50, Q2=4.00, Q3=400, Q4=500, ratio 160, pmax 16, VSI 0.001\n9. DN150, Q1=2.52, Q2=4.03, Q3=630, Q4=787.5, ratio 250, pmax 16, VSI 0.001\n10. DN200, Q1=3.94, Q2=6.30, Q3=630, Q4=787.5, ratio 160, pmax 10, VSI 0.001\n11. DN250, Q1=6.25, Q2=10, Q3=1000, Q4=1250, ratio 160, pmax 10, VSI 0.001\n12. DN300, Q1=10, Q2=16, Q3=1600, Q4=2000, ratio 160, pmax 10, VSI 0.01\n\nPage 5 (Class 2):\nColumns: DN25 (2), DN40 (2), DN50 (2), DN65 (2) → 8 value columns.\n\nRows:\n- Q1: 0.025, 0.040 | 0.0625, 0.100 | 0.100, 0.1575 | 0.1575, 0.250\n- Q2: 0.040, 0.064 | 0.100, 0.160 | 0.160, 0.252 | 0.250, 0.400\n- Q3: 10, 16 | 25, 40 | 40, 63 | 63, 100\n- Q4: 12.5, 20 | 31.3, 50 | 50, 78.75 | 78.75, 125\n- Ratio: 400 (all)\n- Nominal diameter: 25 | 40 | 50 | 65\n- Accuracy class: 2\n- Max pressure: 16 (all)\n- Temp: 0.1/50\n- Indicating range: 99.999.999\n- VSI: 0.00001 (DN25 x2), 0.0001 (rest 6 cols)\n- Orientation: All positions\n- Env class: C\n- Power: Battery 3.6 V\n- Software: same\n\nClass 2 variants:\n13. DN25, Q1=0.025, Q2=0.040, Q3=10, Q4=12.5, ratio 400, VSI 0.00001\n14. DN25, Q1=0.040, Q2=0.064, Q3=16, Q4=20, ratio 400, VSI 0.00001\n15. DN40, Q1=0.0625, Q2=0.100, Q3=25, Q4=31.3, ratio 400, VSI 0.0001\n16. DN40, Q1=0.100, Q2=0.160, Q3=40, Q4=50, ratio 400, VSI 0.0001\n17. DN50, Q1=0.100, Q2=0.160, Q3=40, Q4=50, ratio 400, VSI 0.0001\n18. DN50, Q1=0.1575, Q2=0.252, Q3=63, Q4=78.75, ratio 400, VSI 0.0001\n19. DN65, Q1=0.1575, Q2=0.250, Q3=63, Q4=78.75, ratio 400, VSI 0.0001\n20. DN65, Q1=0.250, Q2=0.400, Q3=100, Q4=125, ratio 400, VSI 0.0001\n\nPage 6 (Class 2 continued):\nColumns: DN80 (2), DN100 (2), DN125 (2), DN150 (2) → 8 columns.\n\n- Q1: 0.25, 0.40 | 0.40, 0.625 | 0.625, 1.00 | 1.00, 1.575\n- Q2: 0.40, 0.64 | 0.64, 1.00 | 1.00, 1.60 | 1.60, 2.52\n- Q3: 100, 160 | 160, 250 | 250, 400 | 400, 630\n- Q4: 125, 200 | 200, 312.5 | 312.5, 500 | 500, 787.5\n- Ratio: 400 (all)\n- Max pressure: 16 (all)\n- VSI: 0.0001 (DN80 x2), 0.001 (rest 6)\n\nClass 2 variants:\n21. DN80, Q1=0.25, Q2=0.40, Q3=100, Q4=125, VSI 0.0001\n22. DN80, Q1=0.40, Q2=0.64, Q3=160, Q4=200, VSI 0.0001\n23. DN100, Q1=0.40, Q2=0.64, Q3=160, Q4=200, VSI 0.001\n24. DN100, Q1=0.625, Q2=1.00, Q3=250, Q4=312.5, VSI 0.001\n25. DN125, Q1=0.625, Q2=1.00, Q3=250, Q4=312.5, VSI 0.001\n26. DN125, Q1=1.00, Q2=1.60, Q3=400, Q4=500, VSI 0.001\n27. DN150, Q1=1.00, Q2=1.60, Q3=400, Q4=500, VSI 0.001\n28. DN150, Q1=1.575, Q2=2.52, Q3=630, Q4=787.5, VSI 0.001\n\nPage 7 (Class 2 continued):\nColumns: DN200 (1), DN250 (1), DN300 (1) → 3 columns.\n\n- Q1: 1.575 | 2.50 | 4.00\n- Q2: 2.52 | 4.00 | 6.40\n- Q3: 630 | 1000 | 1600\n- Q4: 787.5 | 1250 | 2000\n- Ratio: 400\n- Max pressure: 16 (DN200), 10 (DN250, DN300)\n- VSI: 0.001 (DN200, DN250), 0.01 (DN300)\n\nClass 2 variants:\n29. DN200, Q1=1.575, Q2=2.52, Q3=630, Q4=787.5, pmax 16, VSI 0.001\n30. DN250, Q1=2.50, Q2=4.00, Q3=1000, Q4=1250, pmax 10, VSI 0.001\n31. DN300, Q1=4.00, Q2=6.40, Q3=1600, Q4=2000, pmax 10, VSI 0.01\n\nTotal: 31 model variants (12 class 1 + 19 class 2).\n\nModel ID scheme: I could use \"DN65 Q3=100 C1\" style. Let me use something like \"DN65_Q3=100_class1\" or more readable. Perhaps: \"WATERFLUX 3070 DN65 Q3=100 (class 1)\". Let's use a compact ID: \"DN65-Q3=100-C1\". Hmm. Or simply \"DN65 (Q3=100)\". Since class is embedded in tables, and DN sizes overlap between class 1 and 2 tables with different values, I need to distinguish. Let me use model_id like \"DN65_Q1=0.40_class1\" — that's verbose. Maybe \"class1_DN65_100\" ... Let me think about what's cleanest.\n\nThe R137 example used \"G1.6\", \"G2.5\" as model ids. Here the natural model identifier is the meter size DN plus variant when two columns per DN. For class 1: DN65 (single), DN80 (two variants), DN100 (two), DN125 (two), DN150 (two), DN200, DN250, DN300. For class 2: DN25 (two), DN40 (two), DN50 (two), DN65 (two), DN80 (two), DN100 (two), DN125 (two), DN150 (two), DN200, DN250, DN300.\n\nA clean way: model_id = \"class 1 DN65 Q3=100\" etc. Or include the ratio. Since Q3 differs between the two columns of same DN, using DN + Q3 uniquely identifies each variant within a class. E.g., class 1 DN100: Q3=160 and Q3=250. Class 1 DN150: Q3=400 and Q3=630. Yes, DN+Q3+class is unique.\n\nI'll use model_id like \"DN65 Q3=100 (class 1)\". Hmm, but that's a bit unwieldy for JSON values lists. Alternative: \"C1-DN65-Q3=100\". I think something readable: \"class1 DN65 Q3=100\".\n\nActually, maybe simpler: since the certificate type is \"WATERFLUX 3070\" with accuracy classes 1 and 2, and DN sizes, I could do model_id: \"WATERFLUX 3070 DN65 Q3=100 class 1\". Too long. Let me go with:\n\nmodel_id: \"DN65-Q3=100-AC1\" (AC = accuracy class). Or just \"DN65 Q3=100 (AC1)\".\n\nI'll use: {\"model_id\": \"AC1-DN65-Q3=100\", \"label\": \"Class 1, DN65, Q3=100 m³/h\"}. That seems reasonable. Actually the schema has model_id and label and attributes{}. I can put per-model attributes in attributes{}! That's nice — the model_family.models[].attributes can hold the full characteristics per model. But the schema also says to populate characteristics.model_level. Doing both would be redundant but the example in the prompt shows model_level being populated. The R137 example populates both matrix_tables and model_level but not per-model attributes. I'll populate models[] with model_id and maybe attributes minimal, and put the varying attributes in characteristics.model_level, plus matrix_tables for the raw tables.\n\nHmm, but 31 models × 6-7 attributes in model_level means 6-7 model_level entries each with 31 values. That's fine.\n\nWhich attributes vary per model?\n- nominal_diameter_mm: varies (25..300)\n- minimum_flow_rate_q1: varies\n- transitional_flow_rate_q2: varies\n- permanent_flow_rate_q3: varies\n- overload_flow_rate_q4: varies\n- ratio_q3_q1: varies (160, 250, 400)\n- maximum_admissible_pressure: varies (16, 10)\n- verification_scale_interval: varies (0.00001, 0.0001, 0.001, 0.01)\n\nType-level (constant across all):\n- accuracy_classes: [1, 2] — varies by table group though. Class is per model really. Hmm. Accuracy class is 1 for the first 12 models and 2 for the last 19. So accuracy_class is also a model_level attribute. Or I could note it in model attributes. I'll include accuracy_class in model_level too.\n- temperature range: 0.1/50 °C — constant → type_level: min 0.1 max 50 °C\n- indicating_range: 99.999.999 m³ — that's European thousands separator: 99,999,999 m³. As a number: 99999999. Keep as 99999999 with unit m³.\n- orientation: \"All positions\" → type_level string value\n- environmental_class: \"C\"\n- power_supply_type: \"Battery\"\n- battery_voltage: 3.6 V\n- software_versions: list — type_level string: \"4.0.4_; 4.0.10_; 4.0.11_; 4.0.12_; 4.2.2_; 4.2.4_\" — these are software version patterns where \"_\" is a wildcard (any trailing character). I'll keep as a string value.\n\nAlso designed to measure forward and reverse flow — description.\n\nNow matrix_tables: I should preserve the 5 tables (pages 3-7). Each table: columns with units. Columns per table:\n\nTable 1 (page 3, class 1, DN65-DN125):\ncolumns: [\"meter_size\", \"q1\", \"q2\", \"q3\", \"q4\", \"q3_q1_ratio\", \"nominal_diameter\", \"accuracy_class\", \"max_admissible_pressure\", \"min_max_admissible_temperature\", \"indicating_range\", \"verification_scale_interval\", \"orientation\", \"environmental_class\", \"power_supply\", \"software_versions\"]\n\nHmm, but the schema example shows column_units as a separate array. The rows would be objects per column-variant. But the raw table has columns per DN-size group (with sub-columns for variants). Rows are attributes. It's transposed relative to typical. I could preserve the transposed structure: columns are the attribute names, rows are per column-variant (DN variant). Actually that makes more sense for the \"rows[] (object per row)\" format: each row = one meter variant.\n\nLet me restructure: for each of the 5 tables, produce rows where each row is a variant: {meter_size: \"DN65\", q1: 0.40, q2: 0.64, q3: 100, q4: 125, q3_q1_ratio: 250, nominal_diameter_mm: 65, accuracy_class: 1, max_admissible_pressure_bar: 16, min_max_admissible_temperature_c: \"0.1/50\" or min/max, indicating_range_m3: 99999999, verification_scale_interval_m3: 0.0001, orientation: \"All positions\", environmental_class: \"C\", power_supply: \"Battery 3.6 V\", software_versions: \"...\"}.\n\nThat gives 5 matrix_tables with 7, 5, 8, 8, 3 rows respectively. Total 31 rows, matching the 31 models.\n\nAlternatively, one consolidated matrix_table \"Meter size characteristics\" with 31 rows. The prompt example shows one matrix_table for the R137 case. I think a single consolidated table with 31 rows might be cleaner, but preserving the original 5 tables honors \"preserve multi-dim tables\". Hmm. The schema says matrix_tables[]: preserve multi-dim tables that don't fit elsewhere. Since I am populating model_level and models, the matrix_tables are somewhat duplicative. I could do a single consolidated matrix table. Let me do one consolidated matrix_table named \"Metrological characteristics by meter size and accuracy class\" with all 31 rows. That's a good balance.\n\nActually, wait — maybe I should keep the 5 separate tables to be faithful. But the consolidated one is easier to consume. The prompt's R137 example shows a single matrix table plus model_level. I'll do a single consolidated matrix_table. Hmm, but the temperature \"0,1/50\" is a range — in matrix rows I can use {\"min\": 0.1, \"max\": 50} as value? In matrix_tables rows, values can be objects. The R137 example uses plain numbers in rows. For temperature I'll use {\"min\": 0.1, \"max\": 50}. Power supply \"Battery 3.6 V\" — keep string, or split into type \"Battery\" and voltage 3.6. I'll use strings in matrix rows for these composite ones: power_supply: \"Battery 3.6 V\".\n\nLet me now define model IDs. I'll go with \"DN65 Q3=100 (class 1)\" style? Or maybe include ratio. Let me check uniqueness: class 1 DN80 has Q3=100 (ratio 160) and Q3=160 (ratio 250). DN+Q3 unique per class. Class 2 all ratio 400. So \"class1-DN80-Q3=100\" is unique.\n\nModel list (31):\n\nClass 1 (12):\n1. DN65, Q1 0.40, Q2 0.64, Q3 100, Q4 125, ratio 250, DN 65, pmax 16, VSI 0.0001\n2. DN80, Q1 0.625, Q2 1.00, Q3 100, Q4 125, ratio 160, pmax 16, VSI 0.0001\n3. DN80, Q1 0.64, Q2 1.02, Q3 160, Q4 200, ratio 250, pmax 16, VSI 0.0001\n4. DN100, Q1 1.0, Q2 1.6, Q3 160, Q4 200, ratio 160, pmax 16, VSI 0.001\n5. DN100, Q1 1.0, Q2 1.6, Q3 250, Q4 312.5, ratio 250, pmax 16, VSI 0.001\n6. DN125, Q1 1.56, Q2 2.50, Q3 250, Q4 312.5, ratio 160, pmax 16, VSI 0.001\n7. DN125, Q1 1.60, Q2 2.56, Q3 400, Q4 500, ratio 250, pmax 16, VSI 0.001\n8. DN150, Q1 2.50, Q2 4.00, Q3 400, Q4 500, ratio 160, pmax 16, VSI 0.001\n9. DN150, Q1 2.52, Q2 4.03, Q3 630, Q4 787.5, ratio 250, pmax 16, VSI 0.001\n10. DN200, Q1 3.94, Q2 6.30, Q3 630, Q4 787.5, ratio 160, pmax 10, VSI 0.001\n11. DN250, Q1 6.25, Q2 10, Q3 1000, Q4 1250, ratio 160, pmax 10, VSI 0.001\n12. DN300, Q1 10, Q2 16, Q3 1600, Q4 2000, ratio 160, pmax 10, VSI 0.01\n\nClass 2 (19):\n13. DN25, Q1 0.025, Q2 0.040, Q3 10, Q4 12.5, ratio 400, pmax 16, VSI 0.00001\n14. DN25, Q1 0.040, Q2 0.064, Q3 16, Q4 20, ratio 400, pmax 16, VSI 0.00001\n15. DN40, Q1 0.0625, Q2 0.100, Q3 25, Q4 31.3, ratio 400, pmax 16, VSI 0.0001\n16. DN40, Q1 0.100, Q2 0.160, Q3 40, Q4 50, ratio 400, pmax 16, VSI 0.0001\n17. DN50, Q1 0.100, Q2 0.160, Q3 40, Q4 50, ratio 400, pmax 16, VSI 0.0001\n18. DN50, Q1 0.1575, Q2 0.252, Q3 63, Q4 78.75, ratio 400, pmax 16, VSI 0.0001\n19. DN65, Q1 0.1575, Q2 0.250, Q3 63, Q4 78.75, ratio 400, pmax 16, VSI 0.0001\n20. DN65, Q1 0.250, Q2 0.400, Q3 100, Q4 125, ratio 400, pmax 16, VSI 0.0001\n21. DN80, Q1 0.25, Q2 0.40, Q3 100, Q4 125, ratio 400, pmax 16, VSI 0.0001\n22. DN80, Q1 0.40, Q2 0.64, Q3 160, Q4 200, ratio 400, pmax 16, VSI 0.0001\n23. DN100, Q1 0.40, Q2 0.64, Q3 160, Q4 200, ratio 400, pmax 16, VSI 0.001\n24. DN100, Q1 0.625, Q2 1.00, Q3 250, Q4 312.5, ratio 400, pmax 16, VSI 0.001\n25. DN125, Q1 0.625, Q2 1.00, Q3 250, Q4 312.5, ratio 400, pmax 16, VSI 0.001\n26. DN125, Q1 1.00, Q2 1.60, Q3 400, Q4 500, ratio 400, pmax 16, VSI 0.001\n27. DN150, Q1 1.00, Q2 1.60, Q3 400, Q4 500, ratio 400, pmax 16, VSI 0.001\n28. DN150, Q1 1.575, Q2 2.52, Q3 630, Q4 787.5, ratio 400, pmax 16, VSI 0.001\n29. DN200, Q1 1.575, Q2 2.52, Q3 630, Q4 787.5, ratio 400, pmax 16, VSI 0.001\n30. DN250, Q1 2.50, Q2 4.00, Q3 1000, Q4 1250, ratio 400, pmax 10, VSI 0.001\n31. DN300, Q1 4.00, Q2 6.40, Q3 1600, Q4 2000, ratio 400, pmax 10, VSI 0.01\n\nModel IDs: I'll use format \"AC1-DN65-Q3=100\" etc. Or maybe cleaner: \"class1 DN65 Q3=100\". Let me use e.g. \"DN65-Q3=100-C1\"? Hmm. I'll go with \"class 1 · DN65 · Q3=100\"? JSON-safe but a bit fancy. Simpler: \"C1-DN65-Q3=100\". I'll add label: \"Class 1, DN65, Q3 = 100 m³/h (Q3/Q1 = 250)\".\n\nActually, since ratio also differs for class 1 variants of the same DN+Q3? Check: class 1 DN80: Q3=100 ratio 160; Q3=160 ratio 250. Unique per DN+Q3. Class 1 DN100: Q3=160 ratio 160, Q3=250 ratio 250. Unique. Class 1 DN125: Q3=250 ratio 160; Q3=400 ratio 250. Unique. DN150: Q3=400 ratio 160, Q3=630 ratio 250. Unique. OK.\n\nI'll set model_id like \"AC1-DN65-Q3=100\". label: \"DN65, Q3=100 m³/h, class 1\".\n\ncharacteristics.model_level entries:\n- accuracy_class (unit null): values per model (1 or 2)\n- nominal_diameter (unit mm)\n- minimum_flow_rate_q1 (m³/h)\n- transitional_flow_rate_q2 (m³/h)\n- permanent_flow_rate_q3 (m³/h)\n- overload_flow_rate_q4 (m³/h)\n- q3_q1_ratio (dimensionless)\n- max_admissible_pressure (bar)\n- verification_scale_interval (m³)\n\nThat's 9 model_level entries × 31 values each. Long but fine.\n\ntype_level:\n- min_admissible_temperature: {value: 0.1, unit_symbol: \"°C\"}\n- max_admissible_temperature: {value: 50, unit_symbol: \"°C\"}\n  Or one entry: temperature_range: {min 0.1, max 50, °C}. The table says \"Min/max admissible temperature (°C): 0,1/50\". I'll do admissible_temperature with {min:0.1, max:50}, unit °C. Maybe both not needed. I'll do \"admissible_temperature_range\". Hmm, the canonical naming: I'll use \"min_max_admissible_temperature\". Value {min: 0.1, max: 50}.\n- indicating_range: 99999999 m³. The \"99.999.999\" uses dots as thousand separators (Dutch style). Value 99999999, unit \"m³\". Actually indicating range is max indicated volume: 99,999,999 m³. I'll record indicating_range with value 99999999 unit \"m³\".\n- orientation: \"All positions\" (string value, unit null)\n- environmental_class: \"C\"\n- power_supply_type: \"Battery\"\n- battery_voltage: 3.6 V\n- software_versions: \"4.0.4_; 4.0.10_; 4.0.11_; 4.0.12_; 4.2.2_; 4.2.4_\" (string)\n\nWait — environmental class \"C\" — note that for cold potable water class O, M... Actually R49 environmental classes: O, M, C, B, X, Y, Z, W. \"C\" is condensing. Keep \"C\".\n\nRecommendation:\n- id: \"R49\" — canonical. The certificate is under R49-1/2006, R49-2/2006, R49-3/2006. The schema wants canonical id like \"R60\". So \"R49\", edition: 2006, amendment: null, scheme: not stated (Omit or null). accuracy_classes: [1, 2]. Hmm accuracy_classes for R49 water meters: class 1 and 2 (per OIML R49 / ISO 4064). Yes: [1, 2].\n\nActually edition: the schema says edition (int). 2006. Amendment: none.\n\nTest reports:\n- R49-1/2006-NL1-09.01, 41 pages, 14 annexes, role: test_report\n- R49-1/2006-NL1-10.01, 40 pages, 3 annexes, role: test_report\n- R49-1/2006-NL1-11.01, 40 pages, 4 annexes, role: test_report\n- R49-1.2006-NL1-12.01 (typo in original: \"R49-1.2006-NL1-12.01\"), 40 pages, 3 annexes, role: test_report\n\nSchema test_reports: id, date, pages, role. No annexes field... I could include annexes extra. Schema says id, date, pages, role. Dates not given for test reports. I'll include pages and add annexes as extra field? Rule 8 says don't fabricate; extra fields not in schema — better to keep to schema. I'll include pages only. Maybe include \"annexes\" anyway? I'll stick with schema fields: id, pages, role. Date unknown — omit.\n\nRevision history:\n- revision \"Initial\", date \"28 March 2012\" — the schema wants revision string. \"Initial\" → maybe \"0\"? The example says revision string e.g. \"0\" not 0. The table says \"Initial\". I'll use \"0\" with changes \"-\"? Hmm. Rule: don't fabricate. The table says Revision: Initial, Date: 28 March 2012, Changes: \"-\". I'll record revision: \"Initial\", date: \"2012-03-28\", changes: null. And revision \"1\", date \"2012-10-22\", changes \"New software version added.\"\n\nFormat dates: date_issued \"2012-10-22\". Revision dates as ISO too. The schema doesn't specify date format. I'll use ISO 8601.\n\nFootnotes: none present. Omit.\n\nCertificate number: \"R49-1/2006-NL1-12.01\" with revision 1. The full string \"R49-1/2006-NL1-12.01 revision 1\". I'll set number: \"R49-1/2006-NL1-12.01\", and maybe add revision info in revision_history (already there). Should number include \" revision 1\"? I'll keep number as \"R49-1/2006-NL1-12.01\" — cleaner. Hmm, but the certificate identity includes revision. The revision_history captures it. I'll keep the base number.\n\nScheme: OIML certificates are issued under the OIML Certificate System (basic) — scheme A vs B for MAA? The schema allows (A|B). Not stated in doc → omit. Actually the field list says \"scheme (A|B)\" under certificate. Since not present, omit per rule 8.\n\nIssuing authority oiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\". Certificate oiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\".\n\nDate issued: \"22 October 2012\" → \"2012-10-22\".\n\nDescription: combine page 1 and page 3 descriptions.\n\nComponents: The cert mentions \"electronic calculating/indicating device\" as part of the meter but no separate type designations. Omit components.\n\nmodel_family.family_name: \"WATERFLUX 3070\".\n\nNow, should models[] include attributes? To avoid huge duplication, I'll include attributes{} in each model with the key varying values (q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio, dn, class, pmax, vsi). Actually that duplicates model_level entirely. The R137 example didn't include attributes in models. I'll keep models[] entries minimal: model_id + label. Maybe include attributes for completeness? The schema defines attributes{} — optional. To keep JSON manageable, I'll skip attributes in models (or include minimal like accuracy_class?). I'll skip; model_level carries the data.\n\nHmm, but let me reconsider — including attributes per model would make the output more robust for consumers. But it doubles data. The schema example for R60 shows models without attributes. Follow that.\n\nNow units: m³/h — unit_symbol \"m³/h\". Leave unit_id null.\n\nFor value formatting in model_level values: {model, value, footnote_markers[]} — value plain number, unit at attribute level (unit_symbol on the model_level entry). The R137 example shows: {\"attribute\": \"qmax\", \"unit_symbol\": \"m³/h\", \"values\": [{\"model\": \"G1.6\", \"value\": 2.5}]}. So values don't have unit each. Good.\n\nFor q1 values: 0.40 → 0.4, 0.625, 0.64, 1.0 → 1.0, 1.0, 1.56, 1.60 → 1.6, etc. Use JSON numbers: 0.4, 0.625, 0.64, 1.0, 1.0, 1.56, 1.6...\n\nNote: DN100 class 1 Q1 = 1.0 for both sub-columns (col 4: 1.0 → Q3=160; col 5: 1.0 → Q3=250). Yes page 3 row: \"1,0 | 1,0\". Q2: \"1,6 | 1,6\". So both DN100 variants have Q1=1.0, Q2=1.6, differ in Q3 (160 vs 250).\n\nClass 2 DN50: Q1 0.100, 0.1575 → first DN50 variant Q1=0.1, second 0.1575. Q4 50, 78.75.\n\nEverything checks.\n\nNow verification_scale_interval model_level: values 0.00001, 0.0001, 0.001, 0.01 m³ mapped per model.\n\nMapping VSI per model:\nClass 1:\n1. DN65: 0.0001\n2. DN80 Q3=100: 0.0001\n3. DN80 Q3=160: 0.0001\n4. DN100 Q3=160: 0.001\n5. DN100 Q3=250: 0.001\n6. DN125 Q3=250: 0.001\n7. DN125 Q3=400: 0.001\n8. DN150 Q3=400: 0.001\n9. DN150 Q3=630: 0.001\n10. DN200: 0.001\n11. DN250: 0.001\n12. DN300: 0.01\n\nClass 2:\n13. DN25 Q3=10: 0.00001\n14. DN25 Q3=16: 0.00001\n15. DN40 Q3=25: 0.0001\n16. DN40 Q3=40: 0.0001\n17. DN50 Q3=40: 0.0001\n18. DN50 Q3=63: 0.0001\n19. DN65 Q3=63: 0.0001\n20. DN65 Q3=100: 0.0001\n21. DN80 Q3=100: 0.0001\n22. DN80 Q3=160: 0.0001\n23. DN100 Q3=160: 0.001\n24. DN100 Q3=250: 0.001\n25. DN125 Q3=250: 0.001\n26. DN125 Q3=400: 0.001\n27. DN150 Q3=400: 0.001\n28. DN150 Q3=630: 0.001\n29. DN200: 0.001\n30. DN250: 0.001\n31. DN300: 0.01\n\npmax:\nClass 1: DN65 16, DN80 16 (both), DN100 16 (both), DN125 16 (both), DN150 16 (both), DN200 10, DN250 10, DN300 10.\nClass 2: DN25..DN200 → 16 (DN200 class2: 16), DN250 10, DN300 10.\n\nWait page 7: \"Maximum admissible pressure (bar): 16 (DN200), 10 (colspan 2: DN250, DN300)\". Yes.\n\nClass 2 page 6: all 16. Page 5: all 16. Good.\n\nNow the consolidated matrix_table. Columns: meter_size, q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio_q3_q1, nominal_diameter_mm, accuracy_class, max_admissible_pressure, temperature_range, indicating_range, verification_scale_interval, orientation, environmental_class, power_supply, software_versions.\n\ncolumn_units: [null, \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", null, \"mm\", null, \"bar\", \"°C\", \"m³\", \"m³\", null, null, null, null].\n\nRows: 31 objects. In rows, temperature_range as {\"min\":0.1,\"max\":50}, indicating_range 99999999, verification_scale_interval numeric, power_supply \"Battery 3.6 V\", software_versions string. Actually to keep rows compact maybe omit constant columns per row? No — keep full rows for fidelity. It'll be long