": \"The Netherlands\",\n  \"date_issued\": \"2012-10-22\",\n  \"oiml_issuer_id\": \"NL1\"\n}\nscheme omitted.\n\nAlso should number include revision? I'll leave \"R49-1/2006-NL1-12.01\". Maybe add \"revision\": handled in revision_history.\n\nIssuing authority:\n{\n  \"name\": \"NMi Certin B.V.\",\n  \"address_lines\": [\"Hugo de Grootplein 1\", \"3314 EG Dordrecht\", \"The Netherlands\"],\n  \"person_responsible\": \"C. Oosterman\",\n  \"person_title\": \"Head Certification Board\",\n  \"phone\": \"+31 78 63323232\",\n  \"email\": \"certin@nmi.nl\",\n  \"website\": \"www.nmi.nl\",\n  \"oiml_issuer_id\": \"NL1\"\n}\n\nApplicants: [{name: \"KROHNE Altometer\", address_lines: [\"Kerkeplaat 12\", \"3313 LC Dordrecht\", \"The Netherlands\"]}]\n\nManufacturers: same.\n\nCertified type:\n{\n  \"category\": \"Water meter\",\n  \"type_designations\": [\"WATERFLUX 3070\"],\n  \"description\": \"Water meter intended for the metering of cold potable water and hot water, based on an electromagnetic principle, designed to measure forward and reverse flow, with (minimum) 0 D straight inlet and outlet, with no flow conditioner and equipped with an electronic calculating/indicating device. Accuracy classes 1 and 2.\"\n}\n\nmodule_designation: omit (or \"Not applicable\"? Not stated; the meter is a complete instrument). Omit.\n\nmodel_family:\n{\n  \"family_name\": \"WATERFLUX 3070\",\n  \"models\": [31 entries with model_id and label]\n}\n\nLabels: e.g. \"DN65, Q3 = 100 m³/h, class 1\".\n\ncharacteristics:\ntype_level:\n- \"admissible_temperature\": {value: {min:0.1, max:50}, unit_symbol: \"°C\", unit_id: null, footnote_markers: []}\n- \"indicating_range\": {value: 99999999, unit_symbol: \"m³\"}\n- \"orientation\": {value: \"All positions\"}\n- \"environmental_class\": {value: \"C\"}\n- \"power_supply_type\": {value: \"Battery\"}\n- \"battery_voltage\": {value: 3.6, unit_symbol: \"V\"}\n- \"software_versions\": {value: \"4.0.4_; 4.0.10_; 4.0.11_; 4.0.12_; 4.2.2_; 4.2.4_\"}\n\nmodel_level: 9 entries as planned.\n\nWait — is \"indicating range 99.999.999\" 99,999,999? Dutch format uses dots as thousands separators. 99.999.999 = 99,999,999. Yes.\n\nrecommendation:\n{\n  \"id\": \"R49\",\n  \"edition\": 2006,\n  \"scheme\": null,\n  \"accuracy_classes\": [1, 2]\n}\nInclude parts? The certificate references R49-1, R49-2, R49-3 parts. I could add a note field... schema doesn't have it. Keep id \"R49\", edition 2006. Hmm — accuracy_classes: the schema says accuracy_classes[]; for water meter R49 classes 1 and 2. Good.\n\ntest_reports:\n[\n  {\"id\": \"R49-1/2006-NL1-09.01\", \"pages\": 41, \"role\": \"test_report\", \"annexes\": 14},\n  {\"id\": \"R49-1/2006-NL1-10.01\", \"pages\": 40, \"role\": \"test_report\", \"annexes\": 3},\n  {\"id\": \"R49-1/2006-NL1-11.01\", \"pages\": 40, \"role\": \"test_report\", \"annexes\": 4},\n  {\"id\": \"R49-1.2006-NL1-12.01\", \"pages\": 40, \"role\": \"test_report\", \"annexes\": 3}\n]\nThe schema doesn't include annexes but it's useful info; extra fields risk strictness. I'll include \"annexes\" as an extra key — it's factual. Hmm, the schema shows role with options including \"test_report\". I'll add annexes anyway; the consumer can ignore. Actually, to be safe and schema-compliant, I'll skip annexes. Eh... The instruction \"Don't fabricate\" — including annexes is not fabrication. Schema fields listed are minimum. I'll include annexes; it's harmless.\n\nFor the 12.01 report, the original says \"R49-1.2006-NL1-12.01\" (dot instead of slash — clearly a typo for the same cert). I'll normalize to \"R49-1/2006-NL1-12.01\"? Rule: don't fabricate, but normalizing an obvious typo is reasonable. I'll keep the id as printed \"R49-1.2006-NL1-12.01\"? Hmm. It refers to the report associated with this certificate number. I'll normalize to \"R49-1/2006-NL1-12.01\" since it's evidently the same document (40 pages, 3 annexes). Actually the certificate number itself is R49-1/2006-NL1-12.01, and the associated test report would carry the same number. I'll normalize. Hmm, risk either way is low. I'll use the normalized form.\n\nrevision_history:\n[\n  {\"revision\": \"Initial\", \"date\": \"2012-03-28\", \"changes\": null},\n  {\"revision\": \"1\", \"date\": \"2012-10-22\", \"changes\": \"New software version added.\"}\n]\nThe schema example: revision (string, e.g. \"0\" not 0). \"Initial\" is fine as string. changes \"-\" → null.\n\nfootnotes: none. Omit.\n\nDate format: I'll use ISO dates. date_issued \"2012-10-22\".\n\nNow, one more check on the certificate number field: The heading: \"OIML Certificate N° R49-1/2006-NL1-12.01 revision 1\". I'll set number to \"R49-1/2006-NL1-12.01\" and maybe keep \"revision\": 1? Not in schema; revision_history covers it. OK.\n\nLet me now also double-check page 3 table parsing for DN80: header \"DN80\" colspan 2. Q1 row: 0,625 | 0,64. So DN80 has Q1 0.625 and 0.64. Q3 row: 100 | 160. So first DN80 variant: Q1 0.625, Q2 1.00, Q3 100, Q4 125, ratio 160. Second: Q1 0.64, Q2 1.02, Q3 160, Q4 200, ratio 250. ✓.\n\nDN100: Q1 1.0, 1.0; Q2 1.6, 1.6; Q3 160, 250; Q4 200, 312.5; ratio 160, 250. ✓\n\nDN125: Q1 1.56, 1.60; Q2 2.50, 2.56; Q3 250, 400; Q4 312.5, 500; ratio 160, 250. ✓\n\nVSI: colspan 3 → 0.0001 for DN65, DN80(×2); colspan 4 → 0.001 for DN100(×2), DN125(×2). ✓\n\nPage 4: DN150 colspan 2: Q1 2.50, 2.52; Q2 4.00, 4.03; Q3 400, 630; Q4 500, 787.5; ratio 160, 250. DN200: Q1 3.94, Q2 6.30, Q3 630, Q4 787.5, ratio 160. DN250: 6.25, 10, 1000, 1250, ratio 160. DN300: 10, 16, 1600, 2000, ratio 160.\npmax: colspan 2 → 16 for DN150 both; colspan 3 → 10 for DN200, DN250, DN300. ✓\nVSI: colspan 4 → 0.001 (DN150×2, DN200, DN250); DN300 → 0.01. ✓\n\nPage 5: DN25 colspan 2: Q1 0.025, 0.040; Q2 0.040, 0.064; Q3 10, 16; Q4 12.5, 20. DN40: Q1 0.0625, 0.100; Q2 0.100, 0.160; Q3 25, 40; Q4 31.3, 50. DN50: Q1 0.100, 0.1575; Q2 0.160, 0.252; Q3 40, 63; Q4 50, 78.75. DN65: Q1 0.1575, 0.250; Q2 0.250, 0.400; Q3 63, 100; Q4 78.75, 125. Ratio 400 all. ✓\nVSI: 0.00001 colspan 2 (DN25×2); 0.0001 colspan 6 (rest). ✓\n\nPage 6: DN80: Q1 0.25, 0.40; Q2 0.40, 0.64; Q3 100, 160; Q4 125, 200. DN100: Q1 0.40, 0.625; Q2 0.64, 1.00; Q3 160, 250; Q4 200, 312.5. DN125: Q1 0.625, 1.00; Q2 1.00, 1.60; Q3 250, 400; Q4 312.5, 500. DN150: Q1 1.00, 1.575; Q2 1.60, 2.52; Q3 400, 630; Q4 500, 787.5. Ratio 400. ✓\nVSI: 0.0001 (DN80×2), 0.001 (rest 6). ✓\n\nPage 7: DN200: Q1 1.575, Q2 2.52, Q3 630, Q4 787.5, ratio 400. DN250: 2.50, 4.00, 1000, 1250. DN300: 4.00, 6.40, 1600, 2000. Ratio 400. pmax: 16 (DN200), 10 (DN250, DN300). VSI: 0.001 (colspan 2: DN200, DN250), 0.01 (DN300). ✓\n\nGreat.\n\nNow write the JSON. I need to be careful with number formatting: 0.040 → 0.04, 0.100 → 0.1, 1,0 → 1.0 (JSON: 1.0 or 1? I'll write 1.0; JSON parses as 1.0. Actually to keep it simple: 1.0 can be written as 1.0 — json.loads fine).\n\nFor AC2-DN300 q1 = 4.00 → 4.0. q2 6.40 → 6.4.\n\nBuild model_level arrays. Let me write out value arrays in model order (31).\n\nOrder: I'll list AC1 models first then AC2 as above.\n\n1 AC1-DN65-Q3=100: class 1, dn 65, q1 0.4, q2 0.64, q3 100, q4 125, ratio 250, pmax 16, vsi 0.0001\n2 AC1-DN80-Q3=100: 1, 80, 0.625, 1.0, 100, 125, 160, 16, 0.0001\n3 AC1-DN80-Q3=160: 1, 80, 0.64, 1.02, 160, 200, 250, 16, 0.0001\n4 AC1-DN100-Q3=160: 1, 100, 1.0, 1.6, 160, 200, 160, 16, 0.001\n5 AC1-DN100-Q3=250: 1, 100, 1.0, 1.6, 250, 312.5, 250, 16, 0.001\n6 AC1-DN125-Q3=250: 1, 125, 1.56, 2.5, 250, 312.5, 160, 16, 0.001\n7 AC1-DN125-Q3=400: 1, 125, 1.6, 2.56, 400, 500, 250, 16, 0.001\n8 AC1-DN150-Q3=400: 1, 150, 2.5, 4.0, 400, 500, 160, 16, 0.001\n9 AC1-DN150-Q3=630: 1, 150, 2.52, 4.03, 630, 787.5, 250, 16, 0.001\n10 AC1-DN200-Q3=630: 1, 200, 3.94, 6.3, 630, 787.5, 160, 10, 0.001\n11 AC1-DN250-Q3=1000: 1, 250, 6.25, 10, 1000, 1250, 160, 10, 0.001\n12 AC1-DN300-Q3=1600: 1, 300, 10, 16, 1600, 2000, 160, 10, 0.01\n13 AC2-DN25-Q3=10: 2, 25, 0.025, 0.04, 10, 12.5, 400, 16, 0.00001\n14 AC2-DN25-Q3=16: 2, 25, 0.04, 0.064, 16, 20, 400, 16, 0.00001\n15 AC2-DN40-Q3=25: 2, 40, 0.0625, 0.1, 25, 31.3, 400, 16, 0.0001\n16 AC2-DN40-Q3=40: 2, 40, 0.1, 0.16, 40, 50, 400, 16, 0.0001\n17 AC2-DN50-Q3=40: 2, 50, 0.1, 0.16, 40, 50, 400, 16, 0.0001\n18 AC2-DN50-Q3=63: 2, 50, 0.1575, 0.252, 63, 78.75, 400, 16, 0.0001\n19 AC2-DN65-Q3=63: 2, 65, 0.1575, 0.25, 63, 78.75, 400, 16, 0.0001\n20 AC2-DN65-Q3=100: 2, 65, 0.25, 0.4, 100, 125, 400, 16, 0.0001\n21 AC2-DN80-Q3=100: 2, 80, 0.25, 0.4, 100, 125, 400, 16, 0.0001\n22 AC2-DN80-Q3=160: 2, 80, 0.4, 0.64, 160, 200, 400, 16, 0.0001\n23 AC2-DN100-Q3=160: 2, 100, 0.4, 0.64, 160, 200, 400, 16, 0.001\n24 AC2-DN100-Q3=250: 2, 100, 0.625, 1.0, 250, 312.5, 400, 16, 0.001\n25 AC2-DN125-Q3=250: 2, 125, 0.625, 1.0, 250, 312.5, 400, 16, 0.001\n26 AC2-DN125-Q3=400: 2, 125, 1.0, 1.6, 400, 500, 400, 16, 0.001\n27 AC2-DN150-Q3=400: 2, 150, 1.0, 1.6, 400, 500, 400, 16, 0.001\n28 AC2-DN150-Q3=630: 2, 150, 1.575, 2.52, 630, 787.5, 400, 16, 0.001\n29 AC2-DN200-Q3=630: 2, 200, 1.575, 2.52, 630, 787.5, 400, 16, 0.001\n30 AC2-DN250-Q3=1000: 2, 250, 2.5, 4.0, 1000, 1250, 400, 10, 0.001\n31 AC2-DN300-Q3=1600: 2, 300, 4.0, 6.4, 1600, 2000, 400, 10, 0.01\n\nNow matrix_table rows — same data. I'll write rows as objects:\n{\"meter_size\": \"DN65\", \"q1\": 0.4, \"q2\": 0.64, \"q3\": 100, \"q4\": 125, \"ratio_q3_q1\": 250, \"nominal_diameter\": 65, \"accuracy_class\": 1, \"max_admissible_pressure\": 16, \"temperature_range\": {\"min\": 0.1, \"max\": 50}, \"indicating_range\": 99999999, \"verification_scale_interval\": 0.0001, \"orientation\": \"All positions\", \"environmental_class\": \"C\", \"power_supply\": \"Battery 3.6 V\", \"software_versions\": \"4.0.4_; 4.0.10_; 4.0.11_; 4.0.12_; 4.2.2_; 4.2.4_\"}\n\nHmm — repeating power_supply and software_versions in every row is very redundant. I could add a \"description\" to matrix_tables noting constant columns and only include varying columns in rows. But the schema wants columns[] and rows[]. I'll include the constant columns anyway for completeness — it's machine-generated, size OK. Actually, let me reduce: include all 16 columns in rows. 31 rows × 16 fields = fine.\n\nActually, let me reconsider whether to include the matrix table at all — model_level already covers 9 varying attributes; the constants are in type_level. The matrix table adds nominal_diameter which is also in model_level. I think including the consolidated matrix table is still valuable as preservation. I'll include it but maybe trim constant columns (temperature, indicating_range, orientation, environmental_class, power_supply, software_versions) from rows and note them in the table description. Hmm, that violates \"preserve\" a bit. \n\nDecision: include full rows. It's verbose but complete and unambiguous.\n\nActually, you know, I worry about output length limits. Let me estimate: matrix rows: 31 rows × ~250 chars ≈ 8KB. model_level: 9 attributes × 31 values × ~60 chars ≈ 17KB. models: 31 × ~120 ≈ 4KB. Plus rest ~4KB. Total ~33KB. That's fine.\n\nAlternatively, skip per-model label duplication... keep it.\n\nLet me now also think about whether to include model \"attributes\" — no, skip.\n\nColumn list for matrix_table:\n[\"meter_size\", \"q1\", \"q2\", \"q3\", \"q4\", \"ratio_q3_q1\", \"nominal_diameter\", \"accuracy_class\", \"max_admissible_pressure\", \"temperature_range\", \"indicating_range\", \"verification_scale_interval\", \"orientation\", \"environmental_class\", \"power_supply\", \"software_versions\"]\n\ncolumn_units: [null, \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", null, \"mm\", null, \"bar\", \"°C\", \"m³\", \"m³\", null, null, null, null]\n\ndescription: \"Consolidated from the metrological characteristics tables on pages 3–7; each row is one certified meter variant (meter size DN with its Q3 variant). Constant values across all variants: temperature range 0.1–50 °C, indicating range 99 999 999 m³, orientation all positions, environmental class C, battery power supply 3.6 V, software versions 4.0.4_; 4.0.10_; 4.0.11_; 4.0.12_; 4.2.2_; 4.2.4_.\"\n\nOK.\n\nNow