t_number\": \"12200373\",\n    \"page_total\": 7,\n    \"member_state\": \"The Netherlands\",\n    \"date_issued\": \"2012-10-22\",\n    \"oiml_issuer_id\": \"NL1\"\n  },\n  \"issuing_authority\": {...},\n  \"applicants\": [...],\n  \"manufacturers\": [...],\n  \"certified_type\": {\n    \"category\": \"Water meter\",\n    \"type_designations\": [\"WATERFLUX 3070\"],\n    \"description\": \"...\"\n  },\n  \"model_family\": {\n    \"family_name\": \"WATERFLUX 3070\",\n    \"models\": [31]\n  },\n  \"characteristics\": {\n    \"type_level\": {...7 entries},\n    \"model_level\": [9 entries]\n  },\n  \"matrix_tables\": [1 consolidated],\n  \"recommendation\": {\"id\": \"R49\", \"edition\": 2006, \"accuracy_classes\": [1, 2]},\n  \"test_reports\": [4],\n  \"revision_history\": [2]\n}\n\nFor type_level entries, each value must be structured: {value, unit_symbol, unit_id, footnote_markers}. I'll include footnote_markers only when present — schema shows footnote_markers[] in shape; I'll include empty arrays? The example shows footnote_markers on values referencing footnotes. To be consistent I'll omit footnote_markers when empty (rule 8-ish). Actually shape shows it as part of object; include [] or omit — I'll omit when empty to reduce noise. Hmm, in the R137 example for model_level values they omit footnote_markers entirely. OK, omit when empty.\n\nunit_id: null everywhere (uncertain). I'll just omit unit_id too? The structured shape includes it. I'll include \"unit_id\": null to match shape. That's per rule 3: \"Leave unit_id: null unless certain.\" OK include.\n\nLet me write out all model_level values arrays.\n\naccuracy_class values:\n[{m1,1},{m2,1},...,{m12,1},{m13,2},...,{m31,2}]\n\nI'll write them fully.\n\nLet me generate the JSON now mentally and write it out.\n\nModel IDs (short):\n\"M1\"...\"M31\"? No — descriptive IDs better.\n\nFinal list with IDs:\n1. \"AC1-DN65-Q3=100\"\n2. \"AC1-DN80-Q3=100\"\n3. \"AC1-DN80-Q3=160\"\n4. \"AC1-DN100-Q3=160\"\n5. \"AC1-DN100-Q3=250\"\n6. \"AC1-DN125-Q3=250\"\n7. \"AC1-DN125-Q3=400\"\n8. \"AC1-DN150-Q3=400\"\n9. \"AC1-DN150-Q3=630\"\n10. \"AC1-DN200-Q3=630\"\n11. \"AC1-DN250-Q3=1000\"\n12. \"AC1-DN300-Q3=1600\"\n13. \"AC2-DN25-Q3=10\"\n14. \"AC2-DN25-Q3=16\"\n15. \"AC2-DN40-Q3=25\"\n16. \"AC2-DN40-Q3=40\"\n17. \"AC2-DN50-Q3=40\"\n18. \"AC2-DN50-Q3=63\"\n19. \"AC2-DN65-Q3=63\"\n20. \"AC2-DN65-Q3=100\"\n21. \"AC2-DN80-Q3=100\"\n22. \"AC2-DN80-Q3=160\"\n23. \"AC2-DN100-Q3=160\"\n24. \"AC2-DN100-Q3=250\"\n25. \"AC2-DN125-Q3=250\"\n26. \"AC2-DN125-Q3=400\"\n27. \"AC2-DN150-Q3=400\"\n28. \"AC2-DN150-Q3=630\"\n29. \"AC2-DN200-Q3=630\"\n30. \"AC2-DN250-Q3=1000\"\n31. \"AC2-DN300-Q3=1600\"\n\nLabels: \"DN65, Q3 = 100 m³/h, accuracy class 1\" etc.\n\nNow write everything. I'll be methodical to avoid errors.\n\nmatrix rows (31):\n\nRow1: {\"meter_size\":\"DN65\",\"q1\":0.4,\"q2\":0.64,\"q3\":100,\"q4\":125,\"ratio_q3_q1\":250,\"nominal_diameter\":65,\"accuracy_class\":1,\"max_admissible_pressure\":16,\"temperature_range\":{\"min\":0.1,\"max\":50},\"indicating_range\":99999999,\"verification_scale_interval\":0.0001,\"orientation\":\"All positions\",\"environmental_class\":\"C\",\"power_supply\":\"Battery 3.6 V\",\"software_versions\":\"4.0.4_; 4.0.10_; 4.0.11_; 4.0.12_; 4.2.2_; 4.2.4_\"}\n\n...as per table above.\n\nThis is long but let me just write it.\n\nActually — to manage length, maybe in matrix rows I keep all fields; fine.\n\nLet me write the final JSON.\n\nOne more check: certificate \"number\" — include revision? I'll use \"R49-1/2006-NL1-12.01\". Also maybe add \"revision\": \"1\"? Not in schema; revision_history covers. OK.\n\npage_total: 7.\n\nWrite it.",
